   "t": "А я кто?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'And what am I?' Literally and I who? Russian uses ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "кто",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning who, for a person even here, where English says what.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан или сельдерей?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'A croissant or celery?' We can analyze the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Круассан",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " is French and celery is borrowed too, so both are easy to guess. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "или",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": " means or.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну, конечно, круассан.",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Well, of course a croissant.' Vika answers with the noun alone, no you are. ",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "vika",
   "t": "Ну",
   "gap": 0.25
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": ", meaning well, softens it.",
   "gap": 0.4
  },
  {
   "v": "max",
   "t": "Почему круассан?",
   "gap": 0.55
  },
  {
   "v": "narrator",
   "t": "'Why a croissant?' Let's break down the words: ",
   "gap": 0.25
